course: dictionary
lesson: 3534
title: Words with two meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. Febrile is one of them. In a doctor's book, «a febrile child» is a child with a fever, a body that is hotter than normal.
explain: The same word in a news report can mean something different. A febrile crowd is one that is tense, excited and restless. Ask yourself: is this about a body or about a feeling? The clues around the word tell you which.
explain: Febrile is a rare, formal word, so you will mostly meet it in writing for grown-ups. Use the same check as always: put your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
fact: Febrile means having a fever, a high body temperature.
fact: Febrile also means nervous, excited and restless, often in a busy or tense way, as in «a febrile mood».
fact: Febrile seizures, or fever fits, are fits caused by a rising fever in children aged about 6 months to 5 years.
fact: Febrile seizures are scary but almost always harmless, and they do not mean epilepsy, an illness of repeated fits.
fact: A word with more than one meaning is called a multiple-meaning word; the context shows which meaning is right.
exercise: «The febrile child was kept at home and given plenty of water.» What does febrile mean here? | Lost in a crowd | Calm and sleepy after lunch | Very happy and playing outside | *Having a fever
exercise: «The crowd was febrile as the final goal approached.» What does febrile mean here? | *Tense and restless with excitement | Cold and frozen | Hungry for lunch | Very quiet and still
exercise: Are febrile seizures the same as epilepsy? | Yes, they are repeated fits that last for life | *No, they are usually harmless and do not mean epilepsy | No, they happen only when a child is cold | Yes, they only happen to grown-ups
test: Which meaning fits «a febrile mood» in a news story? | Sleepy | Wet through | Having a fever | *Nervous and excited
test: Why is febrile probably about a body temperature in a medical book? | *The medical context points to illness and a high body temperature | It is a book about a football match | It is a book about a holiday at sea | It is a quiet library book
exam: A news report says the parliament was febrile before the vote. What does febrile mean? | Cold and empty | Having a fever | Full of water | *Tense, excited and restless
